### Revised Implementation

Here's a revised version of your `Autocomplete` class with proper event listener management:

```javascript
// autocomplete.js
class Autocomplete {
  constructor(element) {
    this.element = element;
    this.listeners = [];
  }

  addListener(eventType, handler) {
    this.element.addEventListener(eventType, handler);
    this.listeners.push({ eventType, handler });
  }

  removeListener(eventType, handler) {
    this.element.removeEventListener(eventType, handler);
    const index = this.listeners.findIndex(
      (listener) => listener.eventType === eventType && listener.handler === handler
    );
    if (index !== -1) {
      this.listeners.splice(index, 1);
    }
  }

  tearDown() {
    this.listeners.forEach(({ eventType, handler }) => {
      this.element.removeEventListener(eventType, handler);
    });
    this.listeners = [];
  }
}

// usage
const inputElement = document.querySelector('#autocomplete-input');
const autocomplete = new Autocomplete(inputElement);

const listener = () => {
  console.log('listener called');
};

autocomplete.addListener('input', listener);

// When tearing down the component
autocomplete.tearDown();
```

### Explanation

1. **Constructor**: The constructor takes an element to attach event listeners to.
2. **addListener**: Adds an event listener to the element and stores the listener details.
3. **removeListener**: Removes the event listener from the element and cleans up the list of listeners.
4. **tearDown**: Removes all event listeners from the element and clears the list of listeners.

